  "bug_info": "Summary of configuration for Project: Math\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    Script dir: /root/defects4j/framework\n      Base dir: /root/defects4j\n    Major root: /root/defects4j/major\n      Repo dir: /root/defects4j/project_repos\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    Project ID: Math\n       Program: commons-math\n    Build file: /root/defects4j/framework/projects/Math/Math.build.xml\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n           Vcs: Vcs::Git\n    Repository: /root/defects4j/project_repos/commons-math.git\n     Commit db: /root/defects4j/framework/projects/Math/active-bugs.csv\nNumber of bugs: 106\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nSummary for Bug: Math-105\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRevision ID (fixed version):\n075008dcd42adcc6924d048a376e611f9219e52b\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRevision date (fixed version):\n2006-07-06 05:10:49 +0000\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBug report id:\nMATH-85\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBug report url:\nhttps://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/MATH-85\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRoot cause in triggering tests:\n - org.apache.commons.math.stat.regression.SimpleRegressionTest::testSSENonNegative\n   --> junit.framework.AssertionFailedError\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nList of modified sources:\n - org.apache.commons.math.stat.regression.SimpleRegression\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------",
  "bug_report_content": "Title: [math]  SimpleRegression getSumSquaredErrors\nType: Bug | Priority: Major | Status: Closed | Resolution: Fixed\n\nDescription:\ngetSumSquaredErrors returns -ve value. See test below:\n\npublic void testSimpleRegression() {\n\t\tdouble[] y = {  8915.102, 8919.302, 8923.502};\n\t\tdouble[] x = { 1.107178495, 1.107264895, 1.107351295};\n\t\tdouble[] x2 = { 1.107178495E2, 1.107264895E2, 1.107351295E2};\n\t\tSimpleRegression reg = new SimpleRegression();\n\t\tfor (int i = 0; i < x.length; i++) {\n\t\t\treg.addData(x[i],y[i]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tassertTrue(reg.getSumSquaredErrors() >= 0.0); // OK\n\t\treg.clear();\n\t\tfor (int i = 0; i < x.length; i++) {\n\t\t\treg.addData(x2[i],y[i]);\n\t\t}\n\t\tassertTrue(reg.getSumSquaredErrors() >= 0.0); // FAIL\n\t\t\n\t}\n\nComments:\n\n[Luc Maisonobe]: The problem is related to computation accuracy in a corner case.\n\nThe data (110.7178495, 8915.102), (110.7264895, 8919.302), (110.7351295, 8923.502) represent three points on a perfect straigth line, with the second point exactly at the middle of the two extreme points. In this case, the sum of the squares of the errors should be exactly 0 as all points lie exactly on the estimated line.\n\nIf instead of checking reg.getSumSquaredErrors() >= 0.0 I print the value, I get -7.105427357601002E-15 on my GNU/Linux box. This seems quite fair for me as the computation involves computing a subtraction close to 35.28 - 35.28, where both terms result from several former computations. This is consistent with double precision.\n\nWhat we observe here is simply a cancellation effect on subtraction. The result is null in the first part of the test (where the x values are 100 times smaller), slightly negative in the second part. I think the null result in the first part is only good fortune (well, it is really related to the orders of magnitude involved: x^2, y^2 and xy).\n\nI suggest to consider this is not a bug.\nI will add a patch with a slightly modified test case in a few minutes.\n\n[Luc Maisonobe]: patch adding a test case for issue MATH-85\n\n[Phil Steitz]: I agree this is a corner case and the negative result is due to rounding.  The question is, should we force the result to 0 when a negative value is returned by the computation?\n\n[Phil Steitz]: Constrained returned result to be non-negative.",
  "fix_diff": ""
